1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,928
Months after the bazaar, a letter comes to the bank.

2
00:00:02,978 --> 00:00:04,800
It is from Aziz.

3
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:07,752
He writes that his phone was gone from Friday to Sunday.

4
00:00:07,802 --> 00:00:11,088
He asks if anyone used his account while the phone was gone.

5
00:00:11,138 --> 00:00:14,496
How can the bank answer him, and prove that the answer is true?

6
00:00:14,546 --> 00:00:18,960
Lesson 19 showed the block by one operator, and the unblock by two.

7
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:23,424
Lesson 20 showed the messages that the platform sent to Aziz on that weekend.

8
00:00:23,474 --> 00:00:27,768
Those messages are gone from his screen, but each step also left a record.

9
00:00:27,818 --> 00:00:33,720
This lesson shows that record, the audit trail, and how long the platform keeps each kind of data.

10
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:37,368
Every step in the platform writes one audit event.

11
00:00:37,418 --> 00:00:42,312
An event says what happened, in which app, and at what time, to the millisecond.

12
00:00:42,362 --> 00:00:47,616
It says who acted: the customer, a staff member, or the platform itself.

13
00:00:47,666 --> 00:00:51,624
It names the device, the network address, the result and the reason.

14
00:00:51,674 --> 00:01:00,816
Registration, consent, the MyID check, the phone check, logins, blocks, operator actions and messages: each step is an event.

15
00:01:00,866 --> 00:01:02,952
But an event never holds a secret.

16
00:01:03,002 --> 00:01:09,672
It has no code, no password, no document image, no address and no full personal number.

17
00:01:09,722 --> 00:01:14,040
The personal number shows only its first two and its last two digits.

18
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:17,688
So an event can travel to other teams without becoming a leak.

19
00:01:17,738 --> 00:01:24,912
Like the messages of Lesson 20, each event goes into the outbox of its module, in the same step as the change.

20
00:01:24,962 --> 00:01:27,648
Kafka carries the events to two readers.

21
00:01:27,698 --> 00:01:33,384
The first reader is the security monitoring of the bank, and it gets each event within seconds.

22
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:38,808
If the monitoring is down, the events wait, and they arrive later in the correct order.

23
00:01:38,858 --> 00:01:44,040
The second reader is the audit module inside the platform, and it writes the audit trail.

24
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:46,200
The audit trail only grows.

25
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:51,696
No screen, no operator and no part of the application can change or delete an event.

26
00:01:51,746 --> 00:01:55,944
The database itself refuses a change or a delete in that table.

27
00:01:55,994 --> 00:02:00,096
But a person with full access to the database can switch that refusal off.

28
00:02:00,146 --> 00:02:02,880
So the trail also protects itself with a chain.

29
00:02:02,930 --> 00:02:06,264
The audit module gives each event the next number in the line.

30
00:02:06,314 --> 00:02:10,920
Then it seals the event with an HMAC, a fingerprint made with a secret key.

31
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:16,464
The seal covers the number, the seal of the event before it, and the event itself.

32
00:02:16,514 --> 00:02:19,488
The key is the audit key, and it lives in Vault.

33
00:02:19,538 --> 00:02:21,696
Only the audit module can use it.

34
00:02:21,746 --> 00:02:25,680
This is an HMAC chain: each seal holds the seal before it.

35
00:02:25,730 --> 00:02:28,920
Change one old event, and its seal no longer fits.

36
00:02:28,970 --> 00:02:33,024
Fix that seal, and the next seal no longer fits, and so on to the end.

37
00:02:33,074 --> 00:02:36,768
Without the audit key, nobody can make new seals that fit.

38
00:02:36,818 --> 00:02:40,272
Every day, a check walks the chain from the start to the end.

39
00:02:40,322 --> 00:02:44,328
At the first break, it sends an alert to the platform team of the bank.

40
00:02:44,378 --> 00:02:46,200
Back to the letter.

41
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:52,368
The security team of the bank finds the events of Aziz in its monitoring, from that Friday to that Sunday.

42
00:02:52,418 --> 00:02:56,880
Friday: an operator blocks the customer, with the reason phone lost.

43
00:02:56,930 --> 00:03:02,064
Every access grant of Aziz ends, and the message account blocked goes out.

44
00:03:02,114 --> 00:03:07,608
Over the weekend, the app on the lost phone asks for access, and the platform refuses it each time.

45
00:03:07,658 --> 00:03:11,232
No login with his number succeeds, and no money moves.

46
00:03:11,282 --> 00:03:16,656
Sunday: the first operator asks for an unblock, and a second operator approves it.

47
00:03:16,706 --> 00:03:21,816
The message account unblocked goes out, and Aziz opens the mobile bank again.

48
00:03:21,866 --> 00:03:27,504
The daily check found no break in those days, so nobody changed these events after that weekend.

49
00:03:27,554 --> 00:03:32,688
The bank writes back: between the block and the unblock, nobody used your account.

50
00:03:32,738 --> 00:03:35,232
Records like these do not stay forever.

51
00:03:35,282 --> 00:03:41,352
This is retention: each kind of data has its own keeping time, and the bank sets the long ones.

52
00:03:41,402 --> 00:03:46,200
A one-time code stays until it is used or expires, and then it goes.

53
00:03:46,250 --> 00:03:52,176
A registration that never reached level one goes when its time runs out, but its events stay.

54
00:03:52,226 --> 00:03:57,936
The customer record of Aziz and his verified identity stay while he is a customer.

55
00:03:57,986 --> 00:04:02,520
After a closure, they stay for the identity keeping time, and then they go.

56
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:08,760
Audit events stay for the time that the regulation of the bank asks for, and then they go, one whole day at a time.

57
00:04:08,810 --> 00:04:12,912
Policy and text versions stay for the life of the installation.

58
00:04:12,962 --> 00:04:20,496
A retention job in the workers deletes what is due, from the database and, within the backup rotation, from the backups too.

59
00:04:20,546 --> 00:04:26,496
Nothing goes early, and when personal data goes, its events stay with masked references.

60
00:04:26,546 --> 00:04:27,864
Here is a question for you.

61
00:04:27,914 --> 00:04:32,688
A person with database access deletes one event at night, so how does the bank see it?

62
00:04:32,738 --> 00:04:38,856
The next daily check finds that the following seal no longer fits, and the platform team gets an alert.

63
00:04:38,906 --> 00:04:42,120
Each step writes one event, with no secret inside.

64
00:04:42,170 --> 00:04:46,080
The monitoring of the bank gets every event within seconds.

65
00:04:46,130 --> 00:04:52,656
The audit trail only grows, and its HMAC chain shows each change and each removal.

66
00:04:52,706 --> 00:04:56,712
Retention deletes personal data on time, and the events stay.

67
00:04:56,762 --> 00:04:59,976
All of this runs in the bank, on the machines of the bank.

68
00:05:00,026 --> 00:05:06,240
Lesson 22 installs heeyID in a bank, and shows how the team knows that the build is done.
